FILES(1)                       P32W Unix                        FILES(1)

NAME
       files - look through directories and open files, in a window

SYNOPSIS
       files [directory]

DESCRIPTION
       Under the window manager (wm(1)), files shows the files of
       a directory (the one it is run in, or the one named) in a
       window, a line each: a directory with / after its name, a
       program with *, and every file's size. The top line is the
       directory's name. The last line tells how many files there
       are, or, when one is picked, its particulars as ls -l has
       them. The window's corner makes it bigger or smaller: the
       list is as long and as wide as it has room for.

THE MOUSE
       on a file's line    picks it. On the one that is picked
                           already, opens it
       on the bar at the right
                           on its upper half, a page back; on its
                           lower half, a page on. The box in it is
                           as much of the list as shows

       Opening a directory goes into it (.. is the one above). A
       program is started, and any other file is shown by view(1):
       each in a window of its own, as win(1) starts them, in the
       directory shown.

THE KEYS
       With its window in front:

       j, k           pick the next file, the one before (the
                      arrows do the same)
       Return         open the one that is picked
       e              edit it, with vi(1) in a window of 80 by 24
       u, Backspace   go to the directory above
       space, b       a page on, a page back
       r              read the directory again
       q              end

EXAMPLES
       win files /usr/examples
       files
              from a terminal's shell: the terminal waits until
              it ends

       It is also the Desk menu's Files.

FILES
       /usr/src/cmd/files.c       its source

SEE ALSO
       wm(1), win(1), view(1), ls(1), gfx(3)

DIAGNOSTICS
       files: not in a window of wm, when it is run anywhere
       else. A directory that cannot be gone into or read is said
       on the last line.

BUGS
       The first 512 files of a directory are shown.
       What a program that was started writes, or why it could
       not be started, is in its window, which goes when it ends.
